L.. CITY COUNCIL REQUESTS AND REPORTS INCLUDING  AB 1234 REPORTS {\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss\fprq2\fcharset0 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa160\sl252\slmult1\i\f0\fs22 Mayor Howorth provided an update on AB 1272, which seeks to expand a mobile parking payment pilot program. The committee and staff supported the City's interest in opting in, as it aligns with the City's legislative platform and could offer new parking payment options. If the bill passes, participation would not be mandatory and would require future City Council approval. This initiative also aligns with the City's ongoing exploration of parking meter and kiosk options.\line\line Mayor Pro Tem Lesser stated that the City faced a deadline to express interest in being listed as a potential pilot city. Due to time constraints, there was not enough time to place the item on a City Council agenda beforehand. As a result, the City opted in, pending full City Council approval and emphasized the importance of the Mayor\rquote s report to ensure transparency with the community and colleagues. The item will come before the City Council for discussion, but timely action was necessary to be considered for the pilot program.\line\line Councilmember Franklin shared that he attended the Independent Cities Association Conference and found the agenda informative, covering topics such as the homeless update and functional zero, among others.\line\line Councilmember Tarnay also attended the Independent Cities Association Conference and highlighted discussions on the Olympics and its potential impact on the City.\line\line City Attorney Quinn Barrow stated that the Independent Cities Association Conference program materials are available in the City Clerk\rquote s office for public review.\cf1\i0\f1\par
